[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- Introduction -[/b][/size][/color]

RadioXL adds radio stations to Cyberpunk 2077 as stations of the game's own radio system. There is no external player and no third-party audio engine: a station built on it is a real station inside the game's radio, its songs play through the game's own audio on the game's own mixer, and it sits on the dial at its own frequency, in a car, on the Radioport and on the radios in the world. Everything that works for a vanilla station works for it: the radio popup, the dashboard readout, the equaliser icon, the radio hotkey, and the moments the game silences the radio. Tune away from a station and back, and it picks the song up where the station has got to, the way the game's own do.

A station mod is one station.json and a folder of audio. RadioXL reads the rest from the files. No yaml, no script, no sound bank.

[i]TL;DR - your music, on every radio in the game, through the game's own radio. (ﾉ◕ヮ◕)ﾉ[/i]


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- For Players -[/b][/size][/color]

RadioXL is a framework. Install it, then install stations built on it, or build one yourself: the station builder below makes one from a folder of songs in a few minutes, icon included.

[b]Requirements[/b]
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/2380]RED4ext[/url][/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/1511]redscript[/url][/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/7780]Codeware[/url][/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/4197]TweakXL[/url][/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/33442]AudioXL[/url] 0.4.3 or later[/*]
[*]Game version 2.31.[/*]
[/list]
Optional:
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/30726]Redscript Configuration Framework[/url] - the settings panel and the keys below.[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/13378]RedFileSystem[/url] and [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/14139]RedData[/url] - keep My station and the two mutes across launches.[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/cyberpunk2077/mods/31920]RedLogger[/url] - a log of what was registered, in r6\logs\mods\.[/*]
[/list]
[b]Install[/b]
Drop the archive into your mod manager. The plugin and the scripts always travel together: installed apart, the scripts fail to compile.

[b]Uninstall[/b]
Remove RadioXL and every station built on it. RadioXL keeps nothing in your saves.

[b]Source and documentation[/b]
View the source on Github: [url=https://github.com/spuddeh/cp2077-radio-xl]github.com/spuddeh/cp2077-radio-xl[/url]. The manifest reference is [url=https://github.com/spuddeh/cp2077-radio-xl/blob/main/red4ext/plugins/RadioXL/stations/README.md]stations/README.md[/url], and [url=https://github.com/spuddeh/cp2077-radio-xl/tree/main/docs]docs/[/url] holds the notes on how the game's radio works underneath.

[b]Coming from RadioXL 0.1.0[/b]
A station written for 0.1.0 (a yaml, a script and an AudioXL sounds.json) does not play on this release. Its script still compiles, and the log says the station was seen. The station's author writes the station.json below instead; the songs and the icon are reused as they are. If you had Simple Radio Control installed, remove it: its keys and settings are part of RadioXL now.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- The station builder -[/b][/size][/color]

[center][img]https://staticdelivery.nexusmods.com/mods/3333/images/33983/33983-1789596345-412986777.gif[/img][/center]

[url=https://spuddeh.github.io/cp2077-radio-xl/]The station builder[/url] is a web page that makes a station mod from a form. Nothing to install: it runs in the browser, and what it writes is the same station.json and folder of audio described in the next section, zipped and ready for your mod manager.

[list]
[*][b]A new station.[/b] Give it a name and a frequency, drop in the songs, mark any of them as idents, and download the zip. The page shows the station on a Radioport and on a world radio as you go, so you see the label and the icon before the game does.[/*]
[*][b]Levelled for you.[/b] Every song is measured as you add it and its level set to match the game's own stations, a loud one down and a quiet one up, with no change to the files. Turn Auto level off to set the sliders yourself: the suggested level stays shown on each track as a starting point, and a play button hears the track at the level you set. The measurement puts every song at the same loudness as the game's own stations, but how loud a song feels still depends on the song, and a file already mastered to full scale cannot be raised, so give the station a listen and adjust what your ears disagree with.[/*]
[*][b]An icon from a picture.[/b] Choose an image and the page writes the texture, the atlas and the archive itself. Or pick one of the game's own station icons, or keep the RadioXL glyph.[/*]
[*][b]A stream station.[/b] Paste the stream URL, and the page tells you which host the player has to allow in AudioXL's settings.[/*]
[*][b]From RadioExt.[/b] Drop a RadioExt station's zip, rar or 7z on the page and it comes across as a RadioXL station: its name, frequency, songs and icon, the archive included.[/*]
[*][b]Open a station you made.[/b] Drop its zip back on the page to change it.[/*]
[/list]
The page checks everything RadioXL would refuse before it lets you build, and says what to fix.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- Adding a station -[/b][/size][/color]

A station is one folder under
[code]red4ext\plugins\RadioXL\stations\<YourMod>\[/code]
holding a [b]station.json[/b] and the audio files it names.

[b]Start from the template.[/b] [i]RadioXL Example Station[/i] in the optional downloads is a working station: install it and "102.7 Example Radio" is on the dial, playing two short tracks with an ident between songs. Change four things in its station.json and swap the audio, and it is yours. The rest of this section is what that file is doing.

[quote][code]{
  "name": "radio_station_example",
  "frequency": 102.7,
  "displayName": "Example Radio",
  "tracks": [
    { "file": "audio/track_01.mp3", "title": "Example Radio - First Song" },
    { "file": "audio/track_02.mp3", "title": "Example Radio - Second Song" },
    { "file": "audio/ident.mp3", "ident": true }
  ]
}[/code]
[/quote]

[list]
[*][b]name[/b] is the station's identity: letters, digits and underscores, unique among installed stations.[/*]
[*][b]frequency[/b] is where the station sits on the dial, as a number. The dial is sorted by it, as it is for the game's own stations, so 104.9 lands between 103.5 Radio PEBKAC and 106.9 30 PRINCIPALES. Pick one the game does not already use: taken are 88.9, 89.3, 89.7, 91.9, 92.9, 95.2, 96.1, 98.7, 99.9, 101.9, 103.5, 106.9, 107.3 and 107.5.[/*]
[*][b]displayName[/b] is the station's name, without the frequency. The game shows the two together: [b]104.9 Your Station[/b]. A name that starts or ends with a number, or contains the frequency, is refused.[/*]
[*][b]showFrequency[/b] is optional. false shows the name alone as the label; the frequency still places the station on the dial.[/*]
[*][b]gain[/b] is optional, on the station and on each track: a level multiplier, 1 as recorded, up to 4. A track's own and the station's multiply. A raised level must leave the loudest sample under full scale or it crackles; the builder sets a track's level within that limit.[/*]
[*][b]icon[/b] and [b]atlas[/b] are optional: an inkatlas part and the atlas holding it, shipped in an archive of your own; the builder page makes both from a picture. [b]icon[/b] can instead name an icon record the game already has, such as [b]UIIcon.RadioHipHop[/b], with no atlas and no archive. Leave both out and the station shows the RadioXL glyph.[/*]
[*][b]tracks[/b] play in a random order, each song once before any repeats, the way a vanilla station plays. WAV, MP3, OGG and FLAC. A track can be a [b]url[/b] instead of a [b]file[/b]: a live MP3 stream, as the station's only track.[/*]
[*][b]title[/b] is optional on a track. It is the song name in the radio popup; an untitled song plays everywhere a titled one does and its name reads blank. World radios and the car dashboard show no song names for any station.[/*]
[*][b]ident[/b] is optional on a track. true marks a station ident, jingle or ad: one plays after every third song and shows no title, exactly as the game's own station idents do.[/*]
[*][b]news[/b] is optional. true lets the news reach the station - Stanley's bulletins and greetings, and N54 News - the same way it reaches the game's own stations.[/*]
[/list]
No durations, event names, indices, records, yaml or script. RadioXL reads each track's length from the file and builds everything else at load. A manifest with a mistake in it is skipped, and the log names the file and the line.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- Stations that stream -[/b][/size][/color]

A station can play a live internet radio stream instead of music files. AudioXL fetches nothing from the internet until you allow it, in [b]red4ext\plugins\AudioXL\AudioXL.ini[/b]:
[quote][code][Network]
allowHttpConnections = true
allowedHost = ice1.somafm.com[/code]
[/quote]
Use the hosts the station's own page names; one [b]allowedHost[/b] line per host, and [b]*.somafm.com[/b] covers every host under a domain.

[b]A stream often redirects, and the host it lands on needs its own line.[/b] So a station can stay silent even with the host from its URL allowed. AudioXL's log names the host it refused:
[quote][code]'regiocast.streamabc.net' is not in the allowedHost list in AudioXL.ini[/code]
[/quote]
Add that host and start the game again. AudioXL reads the file once, at startup, so each host costs a restart until the station plays.

[b]AudioXL creates AudioXL.ini on its first start[/b], with everything off, and an update leaves it alone. Under Mod Organizer 2 the file is in Overwrite.

A stream station joins the broadcast live when you tune in, after a second or two of buffering, and shows the station's own title rather than the song playing.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- Settings -[/b][/size][/color]

Four tabs in the Redscript Configuration Framework panel. Every station and every song on them is read from the game when a save loads, so a song added by any radio mod is listed with nothing extra to install.

[b]Controls[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Next song / Previous song.[/b] Next draws a song the way the station itself does, every song once before any repeats, and a song you skip to leaves the station's own rotation and counts toward its next ident, the same as one the station picked itself. Previous goes back through what played, and next after that goes forward again through the same songs before drawing a new one. One set of keys for both radios: in a vehicle they act on the vehicle radio, on foot on the Radioport, the metro included. F3 and F2 out of the box. Press a keyboard key or a controller button to bind one; Escape clears it. A key bound in the game's own settings still does what the game says, so pick one the game does not use.[/*]
[*][b]Next station / Previous station.[/b] Move along the dial in the same order the radio does, skipping the stations set aside on the Stations tab. Unbound by default.[/*]
[*][b]Show what's playing.[/b] Puts the radio popup on screen for the song playing now. Unbound by default.[/*]
[*][b]Never play this song again.[/b] Switches the song that is playing off and moves on, and says so on screen. Unbound by default.[/*]
[*][b]Jump to my station.[/b] Tunes straight to the station set on the My station tab, whether or not the automatic tuning is switched on. Unbound by default.[/*]
[*][b]Hold a modifier with the keys.[/b] Gives every key a second row: a key that must be held down with it. A key with its modifier held wins over the same key bound on its own, so F3 can be the next song and Shift+F3 the next station.[/*]
[*][b]Radioport keys.[/b] Off, the keys above serve the Radioport too. On, the Radioport gets its own set, used on foot instead.[/*]
[*][b]Show the song on the Radioport.[/b] The vehicle radio's song popup, shown for the Radioport as well: when a song changes, and the moment the Radioport comes on.[/*]
[*][b]Show the song as an on-screen message.[/b] The station and the song at the left of the screen when a song changes. Off by default.[/*]
[/list]
[b]My station[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Tune to my station.[/b] The switch for this tab.[/*]
[*][b]Station.[/b] The station to tune to. It is remembered by name, so it stays the same station after you add or remove station mods. If that station is not installed, nothing is tuned.[/*]
[*][b]When getting into a car / When the car radio is switched on / When the Radioport is switched on.[/b] The three moments the radio is tuned.[/*]
[*][b]Even if the Radioport is on.[/b] Tunes the car radio even when the Radioport is already playing another station. Off leaves the Radioport's station alone.[/*]
[/list]
Nothing is tuned while the game blocks radio input: in a police vehicle, during a scripted drive, as Johnny, and before the car radio is unlocked.

[b]Stations[/b]
One section per station, in dial order, the game's own included.
[list]
[*][b]Keys step over this station.[/b] The next and previous station keys skip it. It can still be picked from the radio itself.[/*]
[*][b]Its songs.[/b] One row each, with three settings: [b]On[/b] plays as normal; [b]Off[/b] is skipped whenever it comes up, and the next song on that station plays instead; [b]Off while streaming[/b] plays as normal and is skipped whenever the game's Streamer Mode setting is on. Songs the game marks as not streamer friendly start there. A song added by a station mod carries no such mark and starts On, so this is where you set one.[/*]
[/list]
The top of the tab says whether Streamer Mode is on right now, and while it is, every song being skipped for it is marked in its own row. If every song on a station is set to Off, the station plays as normal, and the next and previous keys step through them.

[b]Mute[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Mute station idents.[/b] The station's own spot between songs: its name, its frequency and whatever its host says over them. Each station keeps its own, so this is what silences Pacific Dreams' host. Applies from the next song.[/*]
[*][b]Mute DJ announcements.[/b] Everything that takes a slot between your songs: Stanley's news bulletins, Maximum Mike's song intros and Growl FM's Ash. Kurt Hansen is left alone: he broadcasts over Dogtown rather than through your radio. A station's own spot is the setting above, not this one, and songs are never affected.[/*]
[*][b]Mute the radio when...[/b] Twelve switches, all on by default, and they apply to every station on the Radioport, the game's own included. Each is a situation in which the game silences the radio: a scene, a phone call, a club, fast travel and so on. On, the Radioport goes quiet there exactly as the game does. Off, it keeps playing through it. A switch stands for a situation: turning off the holo call switch also lifts the locks a call brings with it, and the same for a scene and a vehicle scene.[/*]
[/list]
The two mutes are off by default, take effect straight away, and switching either back puts the sound in place exactly as it was. More is planned here.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- A key does nothing -[/b][/size][/color]

Keys are ignored in menus and in photo mode, and the song and station keys only work with a radio on. Check the key is bound on the Controls tab and not only in the game's own settings, and that its modifier, if you use them, is held down. A station key does nothing while the radio plays through another mod's own player rather than the game's, which is how RadioExt stations work: pick a station from the radio itself and the keys work again. If the Stations tab says the station list has not been read, load a save and open the panel again; the list is read when a game loads, not at the main menu.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- A station is not showing up -[/b][/size][/color]

Install RedLogger and read r6\logs\mods\RadioXL.log after a launch. Every manifest fault is reported as [i]YourMod/station.json:<line>: what is wrong[/i], and a station with a fault is skipped whole. A station with no line in the log at all was not found: check the folder path above.

If a station appears but sorts oddly against another one, check its frequency: two stations on the same number sit next to each other, the game's own first, and the log says which two.


[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]- Known Limits -[/b][/size][/color]

[list]
[*]On a news station, Stanley's greetings reach the station you are listening to. A news bulletin goes to one station at a time, between songs, chosen by the game from every news station with a radio nearby, switched on or not: in the city a nearby station of the game's often takes it, so news is dependable only away from other radios. The game's own stations follow the same rule.[/*]
[*]Currently limited to 127 stations in total, the game's own included. This is a limitation of the game's internal handling of radio stations. We are working out the best way to lift that limit.[/*]
[*]RadioXL replaces the game's station cycling, so it cannot be installed beside RadioExt or another Radio Mod that also modifies station behavior.[/*]
[/list]

